course: dictionary
lesson: 1956
title: Companionship and the company of others
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Companionship is the good feeling of being with friends or others, so that you are not alone. It can also be the friendly company that a pet or a neighbour gives you.
explain: «The Rat lets him rest unquestioned, knowing the value all animals attach at times to mere silent companionship.» The word silent is a clue: the Rat and the traveller can be together without talking. Mere means only or just, so silent companionship is just being together quietly.
explain: To check a new word, ask: what word would fit here? Try your meaning in the sentence. "Lonely Sam wanted some ___" makes sense with companionship, because he wanted friendly company and did not want to be alone.
fact: Companionship means the good feeling of being with friends or others and not being alone.
fact: Companionship can also mean friendly company given by a pet or a neighbour.
fact: Companionship is uncountable, so we say «some companionship», not «a companionship».
fact: «Mere» means only or just, so «mere silent companionship» means just being together quietly.
fact: Common phrases are «for companionship», «need companionship» and «companionship of friends».
exercise: What does companionship mean? | *The good feeling of being with friends and not being alone | A loud noise at night | A tall building in the town | Being very tired after a long walk
exercise: «The Rat lets him rest unquestioned, knowing the value of mere silent companionship.» What does mere mean here? | Lost and alone | Very loud | Wise and old | *Only or just
exercise: Tom's dog gives him companionship on long walks. What does the dog give Tom? | Cold water | *Friendly company | A new bicycle | A map of the hills
test: Lonely Sam joined a club for companionship. Why did he join? | To win a prize at the club | To sell his old toys | To learn to swim | *To have friendly company and not be alone
test: Which is the correct way to write it? | three companionships | a companionship | *some companionship | companionships of the year
exam: In the story, what does the Rat give the traveller by letting him rest unquestioned? | *Quiet company without needing to talk | A map of the road | A cold drink of water | A horse to ride
